WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:01.559
Thinking about putting together a

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:03.599
great home cocktail bar?

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:05.669
Just interested in this fascinating,

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:07.889
highly expressive subculture?

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:09.779
Reason's Features editor Peter

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:12.089
Suderman talks with Jacob Grier,

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:13.739
a craft cocktail bartender and

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:16.109
writer based in Portland, Oregon.

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:17.849
Grier is a Reason contributor and

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:19.679
co-author with Brett Adams

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:21.929
of the new book "Raising the Bar:

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:23.789
A Bottle by Bottle Guide

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:25.679
to Mixing Masterful Cocktails

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:27.569
at Home." Suderman speaks with Grier

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:28.949
about the pleasures of making

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:30.929
cocktails the way the Internet and

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:32.489
the pandemic have changed home

00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:34.439
bartending and what lessons

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:36.479
alcohol prohibition still

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:38.549
has for public policy today.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:47.219
Jacob, thank you so much for joining

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:47.639
us.

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:48.869
Peter, thanks for having me on and

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:49.949
great to see you.

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:50.879
It's great to see you, too man.

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:52.859
So Jacob and I are old friends and

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:55.109
it's we're also both cocktail

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:55.889
enthusiasts.

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:57.239
Before we get to the book, I

00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:59.459
actually just want to ask you.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:01.289
This is this is a book that

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:03.209
has a lot of cocktail recipes.

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:05.309
And it's obviously the product

00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:07.349
of it's obviously the product

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:08.429
of great passion.

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:09.989
You are somebody who really likes

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:12.149
cocktails. So what

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:14.279
makes a great cocktail?

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:16.109
Like, why are they so wonderful?

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:18.089
Oh, for me, it's all about just

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:20.279
like the complexity and the balance

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.649
of it and the ability to take

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:24.509
so many different flavors and

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:25.859
integrate them into something that's

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:28.139
bigger than all the different parts.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:29.129
You know, when you think about what

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:30.329
goes into some of the bottles that

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:31.649
we have on our shelves when we're

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:32.909
mixing a cocktail, you know, if it's

00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:34.619
a whiskey, you know, that might have

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:36.389
been like ten or 12 years sitting in

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:37.390
an oak barrels

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:40.019
getting better. If it's an Amaro, it

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:41.759
might be 30 different herbs.

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:43.829
If it stretches, it might be 130

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:45.209
different herbs.

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:47.069
So you get the product of

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:48.749
all this incredibly hard work and

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:50.639
complexity that's gotten put

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:52.169
into bottles for us.

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:53.909
And then in a matter of a minute, we

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:54.989
can make ourselves something

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:56.129
incredible at home with all these

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:57.989
different layers to taste, which I

00:01:57.990 --> 00:01:58.949
think is really cool. And it's hard

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:00.899
that you can't really do that with

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:02.729
anything else, like cooking,

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:03.719
you know, to get that kind of

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:04.769
complexity out of cooking is going

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:07.319
to take you a lot of time and

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:08.249
you know, other things. You just

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:09.418
want the purity of it.

00:02:09.419 --> 00:02:10.918
Like with coffee, I don't really mix

00:02:10.919 --> 00:02:12.029
coffee. I just want to taste the

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:13.649
being as it's made it.

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:14.579
With cocktails, you can have all

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:16.739
this fun taking incredible products

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:18.749
and making something unique with it.

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:20.369
Yeah, I love I love cocktails

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:21.839
because they have character, right?

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:23.339
Like each one of them is a

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:24.929
distinctive personality.

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:26.849
And you can tweak that personality

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:28.649
in the glass. So let's let's get to

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:30.149
the book now.

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:32.099
Tell us, why should we buy this

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:34.049
book? What's it about and

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:35.879
why should people want to make

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:36.929
cocktails at home?

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:38.519
I mean, shouldn't we just go to a

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:40.379
bar and pay $16 for

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:41.969
somebody to do this for us?

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:42.989
Well, if you can find a really good

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:44.819
$16 cocktail, now, you're you're

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:45.869
ahead of the curve.

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:47.789
The inflation it's come

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:48.809
for it's come for our drink.

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:50.219
It has. Yeah. And as someone who

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:51.749
bartenders, I obviously encourage

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:53.429
people to do that and to go out and

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:54.689
buy drinks.

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:55.619
But this book, even though it's

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:57.389
written with the home bartender in

00:02:57.390 --> 00:02:59.609
mind, it really is made for anyone.

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:00.629
No matter what your experience

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:02.579
level. There's more than 200 recipes

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:03.599
in here.

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:04.649
So unless you've been hacking into

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:06.599
my computer and in reading

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:07.469
those in advance, there's going to

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:09.479
be something new for you in it.

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:11.789
But the main idea of the book is

00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:12.749
we wanted to get past the

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:14.639
frustration that I have and

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:15.599
a lot of people have with

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:17.699
contemporary cocktail books

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:19.139
where like Peter, you and I both

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:20.669
have huge collections of booze at

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:22.589
home. A little too much, to

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:23.569
be honest.

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:25.229
And, you know, we want to never

00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:26.829
enough. Never enough.

00:03:26.830 --> 00:03:27.749
Yeah. I'm at the point where I'm

00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:29.429
just like, I'm targeting bottles for

00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:30.809
elimination. Like, I'm going to make

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:31.709
cocktails with this bottle.

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:32.710
And so it's gone.

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:35.939
But even with that, with this

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:37.829
massive amount of liquor at home,

00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:39.299
I would often get a new cocktail

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:40.889
book. I'd be really excited and

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:42.749
like, I want to come home and peruse

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:44.579
this and find a recipe to make

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:46.409
and there's nothing I can make.

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:47.999
And my collection is ridiculous.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:49.589
So if I can't make it, then the

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:51.389
ordinary reader definitely can't

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:52.319
make it.

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:53.639
And so what my co-author and I,

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:55.499
Brett Adams, he and I got

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:56.699
together and we kind of mapped out

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:58.859
this idea of how to make a

00:03:58.860 --> 00:03:59.969
cocktail book where that would never

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:01.169
happen.

00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:03.119
And so what we decided

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:04.769
was we have a pretty basic pantry of

00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:06.149
ingredients, a few different

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:08.429
bitters, sirups, citrus,

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:10.379
easy things to keep around the house

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:11.609
and then with.

00:04:11.610 --> 00:04:12.869
Nonalcoholic ingredients.

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:13.439
Nonalcoholic.

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:15.299
Yeah. And then for the, for the

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:16.919
booze we would take it one bottle at

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:19.139
a time. So with the first chapter,

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:20.759
literally all we do is say, hey, go

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:22.018
get a bottle of bourbon.

00:04:22.019 --> 00:04:22.859
We're going to tell you about

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:24.089
bourbon. And then we're going to

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:26.159
give you nine recipes

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:27.659
that all you need is that bourbon to

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:29.639
make what's in the pantry.

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:31.019
And then we just take you through

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:33.269
the book to up to 25 bottles

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:34.829
where we just add a new bottle to

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:36.149
your collection and say, here's what

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:37.649
it is, and now here's what you can

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:39.479
make now that you've added this to

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:41.009
your bar. And so, you know, the

00:04:41.010 --> 00:04:42.209
first few chapters, the cocktails

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:43.799
are all pretty simple but good and

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:45.149
varied in styles.

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:46.289
And then as you progressed through

00:04:46.290 --> 00:04:47.429
and you keep adding a new bottle to

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:49.379
your bar, you've got a ton

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:50.429
of different drinks you can make and

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:51.599
the drinks get more complicated as

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:52.469
you go.

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:54.269
So it's a one bottle bar and then a

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:55.709
two bottle bar and then a three

00:04:55.710 --> 00:04:56.710
bottle bar.

